        "text": "{\"scores\":{\"center\":4,\"craft\":4,\"facts\":2,\"age_fit\":5,\"continuity\":4,\"delight\":4},\"evidence\":{\"center\":\"Lennon acts in almost every level: he volleys through the cuju hole, dribbles past 'three farmers and a very confused goose,' runs the rules vote, takes McCrum's first penalty, and unwraps Pickles' find. His penalty practice and the shootout game he is building shape the McCrum scene and the finale. Level 6 is the exception: he says 'Not on my watch' and then only watches the USA win, with no action and no deletion to stop.\",\"craft\":\"The story has a strong cold open at the backyard fence, a clear device (the penalty through the fence), a per-level deletion beat, a finale on Lennon's own pitch where the coins fuse into a trophy, and a return home with the pixel message. It sags in Level 6, which has no beat and whose coin is unearned, and in the Level 8 montage, which jumps from 1970 back to the 1920s despite its '1958 to 2022' heading.\",\"facts\":\"Three core dates are wrong, both in the story and in the True Facts list. The Football Association was founded in 1863, not 1847. The penalty kick was adopted in 1891, not 1911. The first World Cup was in 1930, not 1926. The 'Newspapers back in England thought there must have been a typo' legend is stated as fact with no hedge. Other facts are accurate and well woven into action: cuju, the ban tied to archery, the ball used in each half and the 4–2 score, Pickles, Pelé, the traffic-light origin of cards, Dick, Kerr Ladies, Chastain and Messi.\",\"age_fit\":\"At 2633 words the story sits within 2200–3200. The wit suits a 10-year-old ('That's the most sensible silly idea I've ever heard'). The peril is moderate and brief (the stampede and the hay-cart dive), and the static goalkeeper is resolved at once.\",\"continuity\":\"Bit's pixel squeak, the golden coins fusing into a trophy, 'Player One', 'BZZZT', 'Level complete' and the watery pixel eyes all return naturally. The opening recalls the previous adventure ('Remember how the history of video games started breaking?') but does not call back its specific ending, 'THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD'. The shootout game Lennon is building only partly bridges the gap.\",\"delight\":\"There is plenty to read aloud: *Thud... CLANG* off the gutter, the grumpy '...aye,' the pig and the goose, and Pickles wagging 'so hard his whole body wiggled.' The ending stumbles because 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' is printed twice, once before and once after 'The End'. The finale speech also tips into a sermon.\"},\"caps_applied\":[\"none\"],\"disqualified\":false,\"overall\":3.75,\"editor_notes\":[\"Fix the three wrong dates in both the story and the True Facts list: FA 1863, penalty kick 1891 (which also fits 'nets arrived around the same time'), first World Cup 1930. Hedge the English 'typo' newspaper story as a legend.\",\"Give Lennon a real job in Level 6, such as a flickering ball or a moment he helps set up, so the coin is earned and his 'Not on my watch' pays off.\",\"Delete the duplicated 'Saturday couldn't come soon enough.' after 'The End', reorder the Level 8 montage chronologically, and open with a direct callback to the last story's 'THE NEXT LEVEL IS YOURS TO BUILD'.\"],\"best_moment\":\"\\\"No kicking people in the shins?\\\" A long pause. Then, grumpily: \\\"...aye.\\\"\",\"worst_moment\":\"Lennon cracked his knuckles. \\\"Not on my watch.\\\"\"}"
